<h2 class="wp-block-heading block-personal-note__title" id="h-the-secret-to-a-vibrant-no-mush-no-bitter-green-shakshuka-recipe">The Secret To a Vibrant, No-Mush, No-Bitter Green Shakshuka Recipe</h2>



<figure class="wp-block-image alignright is-resized is-style-rounded"><img decoding="async" width="600" height="600" data-pin-nopin="true" src="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/aysegul.jpg" alt="Aysegul Sanford from the front view." class="wp-image-84391" style="width:200px;height:200px" srcset="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/aysegul.jpg 600w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/aysegul-400x400.jpg 400w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/aysegul-150x150.jpg 150w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/aysegul-500x500.jpg 500w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/aysegul-300x300.jpg 300w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/aysegul-96x96.jpg 96w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/aysegul-378x378.jpg 378w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/aysegul-276x276.jpg 276w" sizes="(max-width: 600px) 100vw, 600px" data-pin-media="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/aysegul.jpg"></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Growing up in Turkiye, the classic red shakshuka was my mom&rsquo;s go-to quick weeknight dinner. It is a Mediterranean classic that will never go out of style, so when I set out to create a more modern green version, I knew I had a few challenges to address right off the bat.&nbsp;</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Here are the common issues most people face, and how I solved them to make this recipe <em>foolproof</em>, so that even if you are a beginner, you can succeed on your first try:</p>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>Start by cooking the zucchini to reduce excess liquid:</strong> A common issue with zucchini-based green shakshuka is that it releases too much liquid, making the dish watery. <br>The solution I found was to thinly slice the zucchini (rather than shredding it) and cook it in a single layer, without touching it, for 2 minutes. This seemingly minor step helps develop a bit of a crust and cook off the extra liquid before kale and spinach hit the pan.</li>



<li><strong>Season as you go: </strong>A &ldquo;bland&rdquo; taste is the number one complaint with most vegetable-forward egg recipes. This happens because many people wait until the very end to season everything. By seasoning the veggies in stages as we add them, we ensure every single bite is flavorful.</li>



<li><strong>Use the right pan (preferably one with a lid):</strong> Most versions of this recipe use a cast-iron skillet, and while I am not opposed to it, they do not mention that the skillet must be well-seasoned and maintained, which can cause the eggs to stick to the bottom. <br>If you have one, go for it, but my beginner-friendly suggestion is to use a nonstick skillet to make it easier on yourself. You also need a lid to ensure that eggs cook evenly. If you don&rsquo;t have one, you can use a sheet pan to cover your green shakshuka to trap the heat.</li>



<li><strong>Nest the eggs so they hit the pan.</strong> We don&rsquo;t just crack the eggs on top of the vegetables. By carving out specific &ldquo;wells&rdquo; where the eggs hit the bottom of the pan, we poach eggs nestled within the greens.</li>



<li><strong>Solve bitterness and browning with acid. </strong>It is inevitable that cooking this many green vegetables will cause them to turn a dull brown and develop a &ldquo;grassy&rdquo; taste. The way to prevent this is to use an acidic ingredient like lemon juice (or lime juice). It doesn&rsquo;t change the flavor profile much but removes bitterness and keeps your green shakshuka looking bright and fresh.</li>
</ul>

</div>


<h2 class="wp-block-heading" id="h-ingredients-you-ll-need">Ingredients You&rsquo;ll Need</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The ingredients for this Green Shakshuka are simple and easy to find. While you can find the full list of ingredients with exact measurements <a href="https://foolproofliving.com/green-shakshuka/#wprm-recipe-container-86399">in the recipe card below</a>, here are a few helpful notes from my recipe testing to ensure you succeed with this recipe on your first try:</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img decoding="async" width="1200" height="800" data-pin-nopin="true" src="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/green-shakshuka-recipe-ingredients-1200x800.jpg" alt="Ingredients for Green Shakshuka including kale, spinach, sliced zucchini, eggs, scallions, and Aleppo pepper." class="wp-image-86388" srcset="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/green-shakshuka-recipe-ingredients.jpg 1200w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/green-shakshuka-recipe-ingredients-400x267.jpg 400w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/green-shakshuka-recipe-ingredients-768x512.jpg 768w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/green-shakshuka-recipe-ingredients-150x100.jpg 150w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px" data-pin-media="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/green-shakshuka-recipe-ingredients.jpg"></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Zucchini:</strong> When using zucchini as one of the &ldquo;greens&rdquo; in the recipe, the secret to preventing mushiness lies in how you cut it. During my recipe testing, I found that cooking it after shredding or grating it releases too much liquid, resulting in a watery mess.&nbsp;</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Instead, I slice it thinly into &frac14;-inch-thick half-moons, add them to a very hot skillet, and don&rsquo;t touch them for a few minutes; this allows them to lightly brown without releasing too much liquid.&nbsp;</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Any type of zucchini would work here. Be sure to peel it before slicing.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Kale:</strong> I have tested this with both Lacinato (dinosaur) kale and regular curly kale. I personally prefer Lacinato for its texture, but I&rsquo;ve also tested it with regular curly kale, and it worked beautifully.&nbsp;</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Whichever you choose, be sure to wash and dry your leaves thoroughly; I recommend a salad spinner or drying them well in between two clean kitchen towels.</p>


<div class="block-tip cwp-inner is-style-border has-background has-senary-background-color">

<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Pro tip:</strong> If you are new to shopping for kale and using it in your cooking, check out my guide on the <a href="https://foolproofliving.com/types-of-kale/">different types of kale</a> so you can pick the right bunch for this recipe.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">When <a href="https://foolproofliving.com/how-to-cut-kale/">cutting kale</a>, remove the thick stems. I usually save them to make <a href="https://foolproofliving.com/vegetable-stock-from-scraps/">vegetable stock from scraps</a> later.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Alternatively, you can slice them thinly and add them to the pan, but keep in mind that they are not as &ldquo;quick cooking&rdquo; as the other greens in this recipe, which can extend the total cooking time.</p>

</div>


<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Baby spinach:</strong> To save time, I usually buy pre-washed, pre-dried bags. I decided to use a kale-and-spinach combo since they are easy to find. However, if you want to experiment, Swiss chard is a great alternative. Just keep in mind they take a bit longer to cook.&nbsp;</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Scallions:</strong> I use both the green and white parts for a milder, quicker-cooking alternative to traditional onions. I chose them because they cook quickly. If you have the extra time, you can substitute it with a small, finely chopped yellow onion.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Eggs:</strong> I used four large eggs, which is ideal for 4 servings, with each person getting one egg. If you prefer a larger portion, you can serve two people or use a bigger skillet to nestle more eggs.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Lemon juice:</strong> This is my secret to preventing the greens from turning a dull brown. Lime juice can also be used.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A splash of acid right before adding the eggs removes any &ldquo;grassy&rdquo; bitterness and keeps the dish looking bright green.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Aleppo pepper:</strong> To honor the Mediterranean roots of this dish, I love using Aleppo pepper (pul biber). If you can&rsquo;t get your hands on it, a pinch of crushed red pepper or sweet paprika works as well.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Feta cheese (optional):</strong> I love topping off the dish with a sprinkle of crumbled feta. You could also swap this for goat cheese or any soft, crumbly cheese you have in the fridge.</p>
</div>



<div class="wp-block-group is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained">
<h2 class="wp-block-heading" id="h-how-to-make-green-shakshuka-step-by-step">How to Make Green Shakshuka (Step-by-Step)</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Making this green shakshuka recipe is as easy as it gets. I intentionally picked quick-cooking greens to keep this recipe under 30 minutes. Here&rsquo;s how I make it:</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1200" height="744" data-pin-nopin="true" src="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/shakshuka-green.jpg" alt="Slicing and searing zucchini in a single layer in a nonstick skillet." class="wp-image-86387" srcset="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/shakshuka-green.jpg 1200w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/shakshuka-green-400x248.jpg 400w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/shakshuka-green-768x476.jpg 768w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/shakshuka-green-150x93.jpg 150w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px" data-pin-media="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/shakshuka-green.jpg"></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Step 1 &ndash; Heat the pan:</strong> Grab a 10-inch (or 12-inch) nonstick skillet and heat olive oil over medium-high heat. We want the pan nice and hot before the vegetables even touch it. This is the first step in avoiding that mushy texture.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Step 2 &ndash; Sear the zucchini:</strong> Arrange the zucchini slices in a single layer and cook for 2 minutes. I know it&rsquo;s tempting to stir, but letting them sit undisturbed is how you get that golden-brown sear.&nbsp;</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">After that, saut&eacute; for 2 more minutes until they are softened and the extra moisture (if any) has evaporated.</p>


<div class="block-tip cwp-inner is-style-border has-background has-senary-background-color">

<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Pro tip: </strong>I find that summer zucchini from my garden tends to release more liquid than the zucchini I buy from the store during the colder months. Two minutes of sauteing is usually enough for moisture to evaporate, but feel free to cook longer or shorter, adjusting the heat accordingly.</p>

</div>


<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1200" height="744" data-pin-nopin="true" src="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/green-shakshuka-with-zucchini.jpg" alt="Saut&eacute;ing sliced scallions and garlic with golden-brown zucchini." class="wp-image-86389" srcset="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/green-shakshuka-with-zucchini.jpg 1200w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/green-shakshuka-with-zucchini-400x248.jpg 400w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/green-shakshuka-with-zucchini-768x476.jpg 768w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/green-shakshuka-with-zucchini-150x93.jpg 150w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px" data-pin-media="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/green-shakshuka-with-zucchini.jpg"></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Step 3 &ndash; Soften the scallions:</strong> Turn the heat down to medium and stir in the scallions with a pinch of salt. Saut&eacute; them for about 2 minutes. We&rsquo;re looking for them to become soft and translucent without browning them too much.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Step 4 &ndash; Add the garlic:</strong> Add your minced garlic and keep sauteing. Garlic burns quickly, so 30 seconds of constant stirring is all you need to release its flavor.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1200" height="744" data-pin-nopin="true" src="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/green-shakshuka-recipe-with-zucchini-and-kale.jpg" alt="Wilting a large pile of kale and spinach and carving egg wells." class="wp-image-86390" srcset="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/green-shakshuka-recipe-with-zucchini-and-kale.jpg 1200w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/green-shakshuka-recipe-with-zucchini-and-kale-400x248.jpg 400w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/green-shakshuka-recipe-with-zucchini-and-kale-768x476.jpg 768w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/green-shakshuka-recipe-with-zucchini-and-kale-150x93.jpg 150w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px" data-pin-media="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/green-shakshuka-recipe-with-zucchini-and-kale.jpg"></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Step 5 &ndash; Wilt the greens:</strong> Add the spinach and kale. At first, it will look like a lot, but you&rsquo;ll see it shrink quickly. Cook for about 3 minutes, until they&rsquo;ve wilted, and season with salt.&nbsp;</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Step 6 &ndash; Add the lemon juice:</strong> This is my favorite part of the process because you&rsquo;ll see the greens instantly look more vibrant, and that acidic hit is what cuts through the earthy taste of the leafy greens.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Step 7 &ndash; Create the egg wells:</strong> Use a wooden spoon to carve out 4 wells in the mixture. You want to see the bottom of the pan. When you crack your eggs, make sure they hit the pan directly rather than sitting on top of the greens.</p>


<div class="block-tip cwp-inner is-style-border has-background has-senary-background-color">

<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Pro tip:</strong> To avoid any accidental shells in the greens, I always recommend cracking each egg into a small ramekin first before adding it to the pan.</p>

</div>


<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1200" height="744" data-pin-nopin="true" src="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/easy-low-acid-green-shakshuka.jpg" alt="Cracking eggs directly into wells in the green vegetable mixture and a photo of them cooked." class="wp-image-86391" srcset="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/easy-low-acid-green-shakshuka.jpg 1200w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/easy-low-acid-green-shakshuka-400x248.jpg 400w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/easy-low-acid-green-shakshuka-768x476.jpg 768w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/easy-low-acid-green-shakshuka-150x93.jpg 150w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px" data-pin-media="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/easy-low-acid-green-shakshuka.jpg"></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Step 8 &ndash; Cover and cook:</strong> Turn the heat down to medium-low and cover the skillet. Do not skip this step, as it traps the heat and helps cook the eggs evenly (and quickly).&nbsp;</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">If you don&rsquo;t have a lid for your skillet, a simple baking sheet works as a substitute.</p>


<div class="block-tip cwp-inner is-style-border has-background has-senary-background-color">

<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>A note on timing: </strong>3 minutes yields runny eggs, while 5 minutes yields fully set, firm eggs.</p>

</div>


<figure class="wp-block-image size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1200" height="744" data-pin-nopin="true" src="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/finished-green-shahsuka-with-feta-and-aleppo-pepper.jpg" alt="Finished Green Shakshuka garnished with feta cheese, red pepper flakes, and fresh parsley." class="wp-image-86392" srcset="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/finished-green-shahsuka-with-feta-and-aleppo-pepper.jpg 1200w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/finished-green-shahsuka-with-feta-and-aleppo-pepper-400x248.jpg 400w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/finished-green-shahsuka-with-feta-and-aleppo-pepper-768x476.jpg 768w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/finished-green-shahsuka-with-feta-and-aleppo-pepper-150x93.jpg 150w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px" data-pin-media="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/finished-green-shahsuka-with-feta-and-aleppo-pepper.jpg"></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Step 9 &ndash; Top with feta:</strong> Turn off the heat and sprinkle the crumbled feta over the top. The residual heat will soften the cheese just enough.&nbsp;</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Step 10 &ndash; Serve immediately:</strong> Sprinkle with black pepper and Aleppo pepper, then serve straight from the skillet while the eggs are still warm.</p>


